sub1:assertion {
    <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/claim/morphoeval-economou-palate-ruguga-turing> a pk:Claim ;
        rdfs:label "Economou et al. (2012) FGF10/FGF7-SHH activator-inhibitor Turing stripe mechanism at mammalian palatal growth zones, coupled to quasi-static epithelial plate ridge elevation faithfully reproduces Mammalian secondary hard palate (Mus musculus E12.0-E15.5 & Equus caballus Rugae palatinae) morphology" ;
        dcterms:description "The simulation script(s) 2012-economou-palate-ruguga-turing.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Mammalian secondary hard palate (Mus musculus E12.0-E15.5 & Equus caballus Rugae palatinae) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is the weight of evidence (MorphoEval 0.1.0 overall 91.2/100, audit 2026-10-19-palate-ruguga-turing-audit-v3) and the assessments." ;
        dcterms:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/d58a084/2012-economou-palate-ruguga-turing.py> ;
        pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/d58a084/2012-economou-palate-ruguga-turing.py; output: docs/media/palate_ruguga_turing_comparison.png; organism: Mammalian secondary hard palate (Mus musculus E12.0-E15.5 & Equus caballus Rugae palatinae); checkpoint commit: https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/commit/d58a084; environment: python 3.14.7, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.16.3, matplotlib 3.10.7, Pillow 12.0.0; spec_fingerprint: 14f358af9d03b3980dc4c8f810f623b9c4299743ce9b93b063da6d1923819d25; artifact_sha256: 716777da0d6ff0938c1e277351c739fbfbed969691b961cf0e467f02ef0dce32; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/palate_ruguga_turing/palate_ridge_mechanics_reference.json=2e673658c9722aeb..., datasets/palate_ruguga_turing/palate_rugae_specimen.jpg=a38f6c053ffe9048..., datasets/palate_ruguga_turing/palate_ruguga_turing_reference.json=e76e670dd50c86fe..., datasets/palate_ruguga_turing/paper_citations.bib=fd020997fb3c2db0..., datasets/palate_ruguga_turing/scout_manifest.json=f0a345a5c9039e78...}; supersedes_audit_id: 2026-10-19-palate-ruguga-turing-audit-v2; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Economou et al. (2012): mesenchymal FGF10/FGF7 (activator) and epithelial SHH (inhibitor) form a Turing reaction-diffusion system at the rugae growth zone anterior to ruga 8; rugae are added sequentially 8 -> 1..7b as the palate elongates; a coronal explant cut across the growth zone yields bifurcations / spots whereas a sagittal cut preserves stripes; cyclopamine broadens and FGF10 beads / Spry2-/-;Spry4+/- compact the pattern; Shh expression marks each morphological ruga (Fig. 1) | Pantalacci et al. (2008), Welsh & O'Brien (2009): staging of sequential ruga addition in the RGZ anterior to ruga 8 | Turing (1952): diffusion-driven instability of a two-component activator-inhibitor system | Landau & Lifshitz (1986): equilibrium equation of a thin elastic plate and its bending stiffness; reconstructed_in_script: 2x2 Jacobian and dispersion relation with lambda_c = 3.2221; 1D and 2D RD solvers with growth-zone envelope; 9-stage L/lambda_c intercalation count; 6-condition perturbation suite; DoG segmentation of the specimen shelves (15 / 16 rugae); Pass 3: quasi-static Kirchhoff plate on a Winkler foundation driven by beta max(A, 0), solved exactly by DCT-II, optional chi_m feedback, ridge count / phase-offset / duty-cycle morphometrics, kappa / k_f / chi_m sweeps, 2 Lean 4 theorems on the transfer stiffness; transfer_boundary: The plate constants kappa = 0.05, T = 0.2, k_f = 1, beta = 1 are dimensionless modelling assumptions (no offline-verifiable measurement of embryonic palatal epithelial stiffness); only the sign-preservation, wavelength-transfer and monotone-attenuation predictions are scored, not ridge heights in micrometres | The specimen is an adult horse palate photograph; its duty cycle (0.49 / 0.44) is compared qualitatively with the simulated mouse-embryo ridges (0.42) after the same peak_widths estimator; DoG smoothing broadens the specimen crests | The 2D RD solver integrates on a rescaled x spacing dx_eff = dx (q / 1.24), so the realised grid wavelength (3.37) differs from the dispersion lambda_c (3.22) that Panels B / D use; the plate solver uses the physical dx; does_not_claim: A 3D finite-element simulation of epithelial invagination with large deflections or nonlinear growth; calibrated ridge heights; emergence of the coronal-cut / bead / cyclopamine phenotypes from identical initial conditions (they are seeded by condition-specific initial patterns at L606-633); mechanics-dependent phenotypes compared with a published mechanical perturbation]; other sources: Theory of Elasticity (Course of Theoretical Physics, Vol. 7), 3rd edition — Secs. 11-14, equilibrium of thin plates <https://www.sciencedirect.com/book/9780080570693/theory-of-elasticity>" ;
        pk:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ydbio.2009.09.028>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1038/ng.1090>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.1952.0012>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-213X-8-116> ;
        pk:hasAssessment <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-economou-palate-ruguga-turing-code-traceability>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-economou-palate-ruguga-turing-model-fidelity>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-economou-palate-ruguga-turing-morphometric-validation>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-economou-palate-ruguga-turing-overall>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-economou-palate-ruguga-turing-parameter-expressivity>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-economou-palate-ruguga-turing-repro-package>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-economou-palate-ruguga-turing-source-traceability>,
            <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-economou-palate-ruguga-turing-specimen-match> ;
        pk:weightOfEvidence "0.912"^^xsd:double .

